{
 'title': 'The story of rubber', 'context': 'A lecture in a materials science course on the history and chemistry of rubber.',
 'vocab': ['latex', 'polymer', 'vulcanisation', 'plantation', 'synthetic rubber'],
 'script': [
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "Good morning, everyone. Today we're going to look at a material that most of us use every day without giving it a second thought: rubber. It's in our car tyres, our shoes, our medical gloves and countless seals and hoses. Its history is a fascinating mix of chemistry, accident and global politics."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "Natural rubber comes from latex, a milky white fluid produced by certain plants. By far the most important source is the rubber tree, Hevea brasiliensis, which is native to the Amazon basin. When the bark is cut, latex slowly oozes out and can be collected in a cup, a process known as tapping. Chemically, rubber is a polymer, made up of very long chain molecules, and it's the ability of these tangled chains to stretch out and then spring back that gives rubber its elasticity."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "The peoples of Mesoamerica were using rubber more than three thousand years ago. They famously made solid rubber balls for a ritual ball game, which astonished the Spanish when they arrived, since nothing in Europe bounced like that. Researchers have also shown that Mesoamerican craftspeople mixed latex with the juice of a particular vine, a type of morning glory, and that by changing the proportions they could make rubber that was either bouncier or more hard-wearing, depending on what it was to be used for."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "Europeans first became aware of rubber after Columbus's voyages, but for a long time it was little more than a curiosity. In 1770, the English chemist Joseph Priestley noticed that a small piece of it could rub out pencil marks, which is how the material got its English name. But raw rubber had serious drawbacks. In hot weather, it became soft and sticky and smelled unpleasant, and in cold weather, it turned hard and brittle. Early waterproof coats and boots made from it were notoriously unreliable."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "The breakthrough came from the American inventor Charles Goodyear, who spent years experimenting, often in great poverty. In 1839, he found that heating rubber with sulphur transformed it into a material that stayed elastic and strong over a wide range of temperatures. According to a popular story, he discovered this by accident when a mixture fell onto a hot stove, though the story may have been improved in the retelling. The process became known as vulcanisation, after Vulcan, the Roman god of fire. In Britain, Thomas Hancock obtained a patent for a similar process shortly before Goodyear's American patent was granted, and Goodyear, sadly, died heavily in debt."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "So what does the sulphur actually do? It forms short bridges between neighbouring polymer chains. These bridges, which chemists call cross-links, stop the chains from sliding past one another permanently, so the rubber returns to its original shape after being stretched, instead of slowly flowing like a very thick liquid. The more cross-links there are, the harder the rubber becomes, and with a very large amount of sulphur, you get a hard, rigid material that was once used for things like combs and bowling balls."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "Vulcanised rubber, combined with the invention of the air-filled tyre and later the motor car, created enormous demand. At first, almost all of it came from wild trees in the Amazon, and the trade made a few people extremely rich, while the workers who collected the latex were often brutally exploited. Then, in 1876, the Englishman Henry Wickham shipped tens of thousands of rubber seeds from Brazil to Kew Gardens in London. The seedlings that grew there were sent to British colonies in Asia, and within a few decades, huge plantations in Southeast Asia had replaced Brazil as the world's main source."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "Interestingly, plantations have never succeeded in the Amazon itself, largely because of a fungal disease called South American leaf blight, which spreads rapidly when rubber trees are planted close together. That's still true today, and the leading producers are countries such as Thailand and Indonesia."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "Dependence on Asian plantations became a serious problem during the Second World War, when Japan occupied the main rubber-growing regions and cut off supplies to the Allies. In response, the United States built an enormous synthetic rubber industry in just a few years. Synthetic rubbers are made from chemicals obtained from oil, and today they account for roughly half of all the rubber used in the world."),
  ('Lecturer', 'US', "However, natural rubber hasn't been replaced. It still performs better in some demanding applications, for example the tyres of aircraft and heavy trucks, which have to cope with very high loads and heat. Next week, we'll look at the environmental questions this raises, including the problem of recycling the billions of tyres that are thrown away."),
 ],
 'groups': [
  G('Questions 31–40. Complete the notes below. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.', [
   Q('note', 'The rubber tree is native to the __________ basin', 'Amazon', 'which is native to the Amazon basin', 'It comes from the Amazon basin.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Collecting latex from cut bark is called __________', 'tapping', 'a process known as tapping', 'The process is tapping.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Mesoamerican craftspeople mixed latex with juice from a __________', 'vine', 'mixed latex with the juice of a particular vine', 'They used juice from a vine.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Priestley found rubber could remove __________ marks', 'pencil', 'could rub out pencil marks', 'It rubbed out pencil marks.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Raw rubber became brittle in __________ weather', 'cold', 'in cold weather, it turned hard and brittle', 'Cold made it brittle.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Goodyear heated rubber with __________', 'sulphur', 'heating rubber with sulphur', 'He used sulphur.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Sulphur creates short __________ between polymer chains', 'bridges', 'It forms short bridges between neighbouring polymer chains.', 'Sulphur forms bridges.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Wickham sent seeds to __________ Gardens in London', 'Kew', 'from Brazil to Kew Gardens in London', 'The seeds went to Kew.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Plantations fail in the Amazon because of a leaf __________', 'blight', 'South American leaf blight', 'Leaf blight prevents them.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Synthetic rubbers are made from chemicals obtained from __________', 'oil', 'Synthetic rubbers are made from chemicals obtained from oil', 'They come from oil.', max=1),
  ]),
 ],
},
